{"id":"extend-kubernetes-api-extension-custom-resources","corpus":"k8s","title":"Custom Resources","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/api-extension/custom-resources/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Custom Resources"},{"kind":"p","text":"Custom resources are instances of resource types added to Kubernetes through API extensions. This page discusses when to add a custom resource to your Kubernetes cluster and when to use a standalone service. It describes the two methods for adding custom resources and how to choose between them."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Custom resources"},{"kind":"p","text":"A resource is an endpoint in the Kubernetes API that stores a collection of API objects of a certain kind; for example, the built-in pods resource contains a collection of Pod objects."},{"kind":"p","text":"A custom resource is an extension of the Kubernetes API that is not necessarily available in a default Kubernetes installation. It represents a customization of a particular Kubernetes installation. However, many core Kubernetes functions are now built using custom resources, making Kubernetes more modular."},{"kind":"p","text":"Custom resources can appear and disappear in a running cluster through dynamic registration, and cluster admins can update custom resources independently of the cluster itself. Once a custom resource is installed, users can create and access its objects using kubectl, just as they do for built-in resources like Pods."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Custom controllers"},{"kind":"p","text":"On their own, custom resources let you store and retrieve structured data. When you combine a custom resource with a custom controller, custom resources provide a true declarative API."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Kubernetes declarative API enforces a separation of responsibilities. You declare the desired state of your resource. The Kubernetes controller keeps the current state of Kubernetes objects in sync with your declared desired state. This is in contrast to an imperative API, where you instruct a server what to do."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can deploy and update a custom controller on a running cluster, independently of the cluster's lifecycle. Custom controllers can work with any kind of resource, but they are especially effective when combined with custom resources. The Operator pattern combines custom resources and custom controllers. You can use custom controllers to encode domain knowledge for specific applications into an extension of the Kubernetes API."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Should I add a custom resource to my Kubernetes cluster?"},{"kind":"p","text":"When creating a new API, consider whether to aggregate your API with the Kubernetes cluster APIs or let your API stand alone."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Declarative APIs"},{"kind":"p","text":"In a Declarative API, typically:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Your API consists of a relatively small number of relatively small objects (resources)."},{"kind":"li","text":"The objects define configuration of applications or infrastructure."},{"kind":"li","text":"The objects are updated relatively infrequently."},{"kind":"li","text":"Humans often need to read and write the objects."},{"kind":"li","text":"The main operations on the objects are CRUD-y (creating, reading, updating and deleting)."},{"kind":"li","text":"Transactions across objects are not required: the API represents a desired state, not an exact state."},{"kind":"p","text":"Imperative APIs are not declarative. Signs that your API might not be declarative include:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The client says \"do this\", and then gets a synchronous response back when it is done."},{"kind":"li","text":"The client says \"do this\", and then gets an operation ID back, and has to check a separate Operation object to determine completion of the request."},{"kind":"li","text":"You talk about Remote Procedure Calls (RPCs)."},{"kind":"li","text":"Directly storing large amounts of data; for example, > a few kB per object, or > 1000s of objects."},{"kind":"li","text":"High bandwidth access (10s of requests per second sustained) needed."},{"kind":"li","text":"Store end-user data (such as images, PII, etc.) or other large-scale data processed by applications."},{"kind":"li","text":"The natural operations on the objects are not CRUD-y."},{"kind":"li","text":"The API is not easily modeled as objects."},{"kind":"li","text":"You chose to represent pending operations with an operation ID or an operation object."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Should I use a ConfigMap or a custom resource?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Use a ConfigMap if any of the following apply:"},{"kind":"li","text":"There is an existing, well-documented configuration file format, such as a mysql.cnf or pom.xml."},{"kind":"li","text":"You want to put the entire configuration into one key of a ConfigMap."},{"kind":"li","text":"The main use of the configuration file is for a program running in a Pod on your cluster to consume the file to configure itself."},{"kind":"li","text":"Consumers of the file prefer to consume via file in a Pod or environment variable in a pod, rather than the Kubernetes API."},{"kind":"li","text":"You want to perform rolling updates via Deployment, etc., when the file is updated."},{"kind":"p","text":"Use a Secret for sensitive data, which is similar to a ConfigMap but more secure."},{"kind":"p","text":"Use a custom resource (CRD or Aggregated API) if most of the following apply:"}],"lemmas":{"customs":["custom","customs"],"resources":["resource","resources"],"instances":["instance","instances"],"types":["type","types"],"typed":["typed","type"],"typing":["typing","type"],"added":["added","add"],"addeds":["addeds","added"],"adds":["add","adds"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"extensions":["extension","extensions"],"pages":["page","pages"],"paging":["paging","page"],"di